---
import PostLayout from '@/layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import CoherenceDemo from '@/components/blog/CoherenceDemo.astro';
import WalkerDemo from '@/components/blog/WalkerDemo.astro';
import DetectionChart from '@/components/blog/DetectionChart.astro';
import SpectrumChart from '@/components/blog/SpectrumChart.astro';
import CrossoverChart from '@/components/blog/CrossoverChart.astro';
import BodyNetwork from '@/components/blog/BodyNetwork.astro';
import { posts } from '@/data/posts';
import '@/styles/viz.css';

const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === 'fake-motion-fingerprint')!;
---
<PostLayout title={post.title} date={new Date(post.date)} description={post.description} readingMinutes={post.readingMinutes}>
  <p>
    AI-generated images carry fingerprints. Look at them in the frequency domain and many generators leave
    regular traces that a simple classifier can pick up<a href="#ref-frank" class="cite">[1]</a>.
    Generated <em>motion</em>, for game characters, avatars and digital humans, is getting good too.
    So I wondered: does it have a fingerprint as well? And does that fingerprint survive the cleanup filtering
    every motion-capture pipeline applies anyway?
  </p>
  <p>
    To find out, I picked the model I know best: ZeroEGGS, a speech-to-gesture model I developed with colleagues
    at Ubisoft La Forge<a href="#ref-zeggs" class="cite">[2]</a>. My hypothesis was half wrong, and the half that
    was wrong turned out to be the more interesting part.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to fingerprint motion</h2>
  <p>
    Treat each joint's position over time as a signal and split it into tempos: slow sway, the 1–3 Hz rhythm of
    gesture strokes, quick twitches and beats. Then there are two natural things to measure.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Energy:</strong> how much each joint moves at each tempo, its power spectrum. This is the motion
      version of the image trick.</li>
    <li><strong>Coordination:</strong> how steadily two joints keep time with each other at each tempo. The
      standard measure is <em>coherence</em>, plus the timing offset between the pair.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    My bet was on coordination. A generator can learn what a good elbow motion looks like, and what a good wrist
    motion looks like, without learning that they move as one arm. Coherence also has two properties that matter
    here. It ignores how big a movement is, and filtering every joint with the same filter leaves it unchanged,
    as long as some signal survives. Energy has neither property.
  </p>

  <CoherenceDemo />

  <p>
    The question itself isn't new. In 2005, Ren, Patrick, Efros, Hodgins and Rehg trained statistical models to
    tell natural motion from keyframed, edited and noisy motion<a href="#ref-ren" class="cite">[3]</a>. Spectra
    are used to score predicted motion<a href="#ref-npss" class="cite">[4]</a>, and there are detectors for
    AI-generated <em>videos</em> of people<a href="#ref-farid" class="cite">[5]</a>. What I couldn't find was a
    frequency fingerprint applied to skeleton motion from a modern generator and tested against cleanup.
  </p>

  <h2>First, a toy walker</h2>
  <p>
    Before touching real data I built a simulated walker: ten joint angles driven by one shared stride rhythm,
    plus postural sway, a little tremor and sensor noise. Then I made five kinds of fake walk, and one control
    that is real motion low-pass filtered at 6 Hz, the way studios clean mocap. A good detector should
    <em>not</em> flag that one.
  </p>

  <WalkerDemo />

  <p>
    The detector only ever sees real walks. It learns what their fingerprints look like and flags anything that
    doesn't fit, which is the realistic setting when you don't know which generator you're up against.
  </p>

  <DetectionChart mode="toy" />

  <p>
    The two fingerprints fail in opposite places. Energy flags every cleaned real walk as fake, and misses the
    Frankenstein, learned-style and wrong-arm walkers almost entirely. Coordination ignores the cleanup (0.52)
    and catches all three (1.00). Jitter is the one fake energy handles better, which makes sense: it adds
    movement without changing much about timing.
  </p>
  <p>Two lessons from the toy carried over to everything after it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You can only measure coordination where there's signal.</strong> Above about twice the cleanup
      cutoff the filtered motion is essentially gone, and what's left is leakage from the big stride motion. In
      my first run that alone made coordination flag every cleaned walk. So the analysis stops below that point.</li>
    <li><strong>Steady isn't the same as right.</strong> Coherence only checks that two joints keep a steady
      relationship. Arms swinging with the wrong leg are perfectly steady, so the fingerprint also records each
      pair's timing offset.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The toy has an obvious limit: I built the "real" motion myself, so it shows how the method works, not that
    real generators fail this way. For that I needed real data.
  </p>

  <h2>Real gestures: the actor vs ZeroEGGS</h2>
  <p>
    The ZEGGS dataset has about 2¼ hours of one actor speaking in 19 styles, from neutral to sarcastic to
    laughing<a href="#ref-zeggs" class="cite">[2]</a>. ZeroEGGS was trained on 46 of its 67 takes and never saw
    the other 21. That gives a clean test. For each unseen take I fed its audio to the pretrained model, with a
    style example from a <em>different</em> take of the same style, so the only thing taken from the unseen take
    was its speech. I compared 30-second windows of eleven upper-body joints, from chest and head out to the
    wrists.
  </p>
  <p>
    I kept the two controls. Cleaned real motion should pass, and a Frankenstein built from the actor's joints
    across different takes should be caught. If those misbehave, nothing else can be trusted.
  </p>

  <DetectionChart mode="real" />

  <p>
    The controls behave: coordination ignores cleanup and catches every Frankenstein window. But the ZeroEGGS row
    did not go the way I expected. The detector that only knows real motion catches ZeroEGGS best by
    <strong>energy shape</strong> (0.82), the energy fingerprint with each joint's overall size divided out.
    Coordination scores only 0.62, and its interval includes chance.
  </p>
  <p>The spectrum shows why.</p>

  <SpectrumChart />

  <p>
    ZeroEGGS is too smooth. Across all joints it has about 40× less movement at 4–8 Hz than the actor: the
    quick, small motions of a real body mostly aren't there. That's a familiar trait of learned motion models,
    which tend to average away fast detail, and it's the cheapest giveaway there is.
  </p>

  <h2>So is coordination fine?</h2>
  <p>
    Not quite. My real-only detector averages about 2,500 coordination numbers per window, and a difference
    concentrated in a few joint pairs gets lost in that average. A classifier trained with labelled examples is the
    fairest test of whether a difference exists at all. Tested only on takes it never saw, it separates ZeroEGGS
    from the actor best with <strong>coordination: 0.99</strong>, ahead of energy (0.95) and energy shape
    (0.94). It also confirms the toy's warning: from energy it flags cleaned real motion as fake (0.77), from
    coordination barely (0.56).
  </p>
  <p>The difference has a clear shape.</p>

  <CrossoverChart />

  <p>
    At slow tempos ZeroEGGS keeps the upper body <em>more</em> in lockstep than the actor does. At fast tempos it
    keeps it <em>less</em>. Drawn on the body it looks like this:
  </p>

  <BodyNetwork />

  <p>
    In the slowest band the elbows are the story. In ZeroEGGS they move with the chest and the opposite shoulder
    about twice as steadily as in the actor (right elbow and chest: 0.19 for the actor, 0.39 for ZeroEGGS). In the
    fastest band the head stands out: its quick movements are less tied to the shoulders and arms (head and left
    shoulder: 0.67 for the actor, 0.55 for ZeroEGGS). The flip is near-total: in the slowest band 38 of the 55
    pairs are clearly more in lockstep than the actor and only 4 less; in the fastest band 37 are clearly more
    independent and only 5 more locked.
  </p>
  <p>
    My reading, and it is a reading rather than a result: the model moves the arms and torso more as one block on
    slow time scales, where a person's slow drifts are more independent. On fast time scales a person's quick
    movements, a beat or a laugh, ripple through head and shoulders together, while the model's fast movements are
    weaker and less shared. Put simply, real people are loose when slow and tightly linked when fast; ZeroEGGS is
    the other way round.
  </p>

  <h2>What I got wrong along the way</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The hypothesis.</strong> I expected coordination to be the giveaway. Without examples of fakes,
      smoothness is the easier one; coordination differences are real but subtler.</li>
    <li><strong>My first toy fake was caught for the wrong reason.</strong> Scrambling each joint's timing with
      a standard trick also injected broadband noise from the clip's edges, so energy "caught" it through an
      artifact. I replaced it with the Frankenstein walker.</li>
    <li><strong>My first detector was too blunt.</strong> Averaging thousands of coordination numbers hid real
      differences, which the trained classifier then found.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    I also checked the things that could fake these results. The two skeletons have identical bone lengths, so the
    smaller ZeroEGGS motion isn't a units mix-up. And ZeroEGGS's weak fast motion sits far above any noise floor,
    so noise can't explain its lower fast-tempo coherence.
  </p>

  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One actor, one model, one random seed. This is a case study of ZeroEGGS, not a claim about generated
      motion in general.</li>
    <li>The cleanup control is weak on this data: the mocap was already smooth above 6 Hz, so a 6 Hz filter
      changes little.</li>
    <li>The Speech style has no training takes, so its three takes used another unseen Speech take as the style
      example.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>To spot generated motion without examples of it,</strong> check smoothness first. For now it's
      the cheapest tell.</li>
    <li><strong>To compare motion across cleanup pipelines,</strong> prefer coordination. Energy confuses
      cleaning with faking; coherence doesn't, as long as you stay where the signal survives.</li>
    <li><strong>To improve a generator,</strong> look at coordination across tempos. For ZeroEGGS it points at
      something specific: loosen the slow coupling between arms and torso, and let fast movements travel through
      the upper body.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
  <ol class="refs">
    <li id="ref-frank">J. Frank, T. Eisenhofer, L. Schönherr, A. Fischer, D. Kolossa, T. Holz.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.08685">Leveraging Frequency Analysis for Deep Fake Image Recognition</a>. ICML 2020.</li>
    <li id="ref-zeggs">S. Ghorbani, Y. Ferstl, D. Holden, N. F. Troje, M.-A. Carbonneau.
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/cgf.14734">ZeroEGGS: Zero-shot Example-based Gesture Generation from Speech</a>.
      Computer Graphics Forum, 2023. Dataset and model: <a href="https://github.com/ubisoft/ubisoft-laforge-ZeroEGGS">github.com/ubisoft/ubisoft-laforge-ZeroEGGS</a>.</li>
    <li id="ref-ren">L. Ren, A. Patrick, A. A. Efros, J. K. Hodgins, J. M. Rehg.
      <a href="https://www.ri.cmu.edu/publications/a-data-driven-approach-to-quantifying-natural-human-motion">A Data-Driven Approach to Quantifying Natural Human Motion</a>. SIGGRAPH 2005.</li>
    <li id="ref-npss">A. Gopalakrishnan, A. Mali, D. Kifer, C. L. Giles, A. G. Ororbia.
      <a href="https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content_CVPR_2019/html/Gopalakrishnan_A_Neural_Temporal_Model_for_Human_Motion_Prediction_CVPR_2019_paper.html">A Neural Temporal Model for Human Motion Prediction</a>. CVPR 2019.</li>
    <li id="ref-farid">M. Boháček, H. Farid.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.00526">Human Action CLIPs: Detecting AI-generated Human Motion</a>. 2024.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="license-note">
    The ZEGGS dataset and the ZeroEGGS model are © Ubisoft and licensed CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. The figures here show
    statistics computed from them; no motion from the dataset is reproduced. The walkers are a simulation.
  </p>

  <style>
    .cite { font-size: 0.72em; vertical-align: super; margin-left: 0.1em; text-decoration: none !important; }
    .refs { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--text-muted); }
    .refs li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .license-note { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: var(--space-md); }
  </style>
</PostLayout>
